WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:03.259
All right, let's get into audience questions
for the week.

00:00:03.259 --> 00:00:07.359
This one I got in many forms from a lot of
different people.

00:00:07.359 --> 00:00:14.450
How the hell did Trump gain nearly 10 million
votes since 2016, given the disaster of his

00:00:14.450 --> 00:00:15.450
presidency?

00:00:15.450 --> 00:00:18.870
This is a really interesting one because there's
a couple of different angles to it now.

00:00:18.870 --> 00:00:24.200
At the same time that there are people on
the left saying how on earth would more people

00:00:24.200 --> 00:00:26.660
vote for Trump this time than last time?

00:00:26.660 --> 00:00:31.610
You have people on the right saying clearly
there was election fraud because there's no

00:00:31.610 --> 00:00:37.390
way Joe Biden would get so many more votes
than Trump had in 2016, never mind more votes

00:00:37.390 --> 00:00:38.760
than Trump had in 2010.

00:00:38.760 --> 00:00:42.729
So you do have people on both sides just looking
at the popular vote numbers and saying this

00:00:42.729 --> 00:00:45.070
is something about this doesn't make sense.

00:00:45.070 --> 00:00:50.809
The first thing before we even talk about
politics and and pandemic economy, all of

00:00:50.809 --> 00:00:56.260
these different things, it turns out that
when it's easier to vote.

00:00:56.260 --> 00:00:57.610
More people vote.

00:00:57.610 --> 00:01:02.579
That's what we learned undoubtedly in this
election and when you had increased early

00:01:02.579 --> 00:01:07.700
voting and most importantly, allowing in just
about every state anybody who wants an absentee

00:01:07.700 --> 00:01:11.950
ballot to get an absentee ballot and to be
able to vote by mail because of the pandemic,

00:01:11.950 --> 00:01:16.810
because of the risk of catching coronavirus,
where previously many states said if you want

00:01:16.810 --> 00:01:20.649
an absentee ballot, it has to be one of these
rules.

00:01:20.649 --> 00:01:22.119
You physically can't make it.

00:01:22.119 --> 00:01:24.340
You're out of the country, you know, whatever.

00:01:24.340 --> 00:01:30.689
We change that logically because of the pandemic
to be much more open to whoever wants to just

00:01:30.689 --> 00:01:32.600
not go to the polls.

00:01:32.600 --> 00:01:35.330
And it turns out that we were right all along.

00:01:35.330 --> 00:01:36.719
The left was right all along.

00:01:36.719 --> 00:01:40.180
There is a desire for more voting in the United
States.

00:01:40.180 --> 00:01:42.659
Not everybody, not 100 percent turnout.

00:01:42.659 --> 00:01:45.930
But if you make it easier for people to vote,
more people vote.

00:01:45.930 --> 00:01:50.070
And that's not a particularly good thing for
the Republican Party, but that's part one.

00:01:50.070 --> 00:01:55.030
So both Donald Trump and Joe Biden got huge
amounts of votes never before seen in any

00:01:55.030 --> 00:01:56.640
presidential election.

00:01:56.640 --> 00:02:04.350
Then the question becomes a political question,
which is given that Trump had his 2016 base.

00:02:04.350 --> 00:02:05.869
And he screwed everything up.

00:02:05.869 --> 00:02:12.140
I mean, he screwed up health care, pandemic
response, trade policy, immigration policy,

00:02:12.140 --> 00:02:16.110
you know, we could go on and on all day.

00:02:16.110 --> 00:02:21.380
How did he get more people to vote for him
this time around?

00:02:21.380 --> 00:02:25.330
And there's a couple of different things that
that we have to account for know.

00:02:25.330 --> 00:02:26.740
One of these is the answer.

00:02:26.740 --> 00:02:31.180
Number one, the power of incumbency is a real
thing.

00:02:31.180 --> 00:02:36.440
When we say it's hard to knock off an incumbent
president, it's because it's very hard to

00:02:36.440 --> 00:02:40.160
knock off an incumbent president when you
are the president and you're flying around

00:02:40.160 --> 00:02:45.930
in Air Force One and people are comparing
a president to a non president, that will

00:02:45.930 --> 00:02:49.880
counteract a lot of the reasons that if you
were merely a challenger, people might not

00:02:49.880 --> 00:02:51.280
vote for you.

00:02:51.280 --> 00:02:57.860
This risk averse don't change horses midstream,
particularly for people who are not very politically

00:02:57.860 --> 00:02:58.860
engaged.

00:02:58.860 --> 00:03:03.710
It takes a lot more to get people over the
hump to vote to remove the existing president.

00:03:03.710 --> 00:03:06.860
That's just a reality and that's a part of
it.

00:03:06.860 --> 00:03:09.300
The other thing is there are people who wrote
to me.

00:03:09.300 --> 00:03:15.520
They said, you know, one of the things that
happened is almost inadvertently, Trump lan

00:03:15.520 --> 00:03:21.830
ran less and did less racist stuff in the
last six to 12 months because of his focus

00:03:21.830 --> 00:03:23.320
on the virus.

00:03:23.320 --> 00:03:25.000
I don't know that that's the case.

00:03:25.000 --> 00:03:33.680
I mean, first of all, from the China Plague
Kong flu stuff to Cory Booker, black senator

00:03:33.680 --> 00:03:38.900
will end the suburbs and minorities are going
to pour in and the suburbs, as you know them

00:03:38.900 --> 00:03:41.070
all, stop low income housing.

00:03:41.070 --> 00:03:44.010
There were plenty of racist dog whistles going
on.

00:03:44.010 --> 00:03:51.220
But what is true is that there was less Hispanic
related xenophobia.

00:03:51.220 --> 00:03:55.910
Maybe you could make the case Trump didn't
run as much on the wall.

00:03:55.910 --> 00:04:00.980
Remember, the 2016 campaign was launched in
2015 on Trump coming down the escalator and

00:04:00.980 --> 00:04:03.840
saying Mexico's not sending their best.

00:04:03.840 --> 00:04:08.620
We're going to stop the rapists and all these
people from coming over.

00:04:08.620 --> 00:04:16.549
Trump was less overtly about that because
he was very worried about other types of xenophobia.

00:04:16.549 --> 00:04:21.790
And case in point, Trump did end up with a
larger share of the Hispanic vote in 2020

00:04:21.790 --> 00:04:23.380
than he did in 2016.

00:04:23.380 --> 00:04:25.640
So I think that that's a factor.

00:04:25.640 --> 00:04:33.900
The other idea I have is could the virus,
as as disastrous as Trump's handling was of

00:04:33.900 --> 00:04:41.740
the virus, could it have somehow in a backwards
way helped Trump with minority voters in that

00:04:41.740 --> 00:04:48.320
there are many working class folks, including
those without college degrees?

00:04:48.320 --> 00:04:52.460
This applies both to white voters without
college degrees and minority voters.

00:04:52.460 --> 00:04:58.000
But Trump already had the white voters without
college degrees who saw, hey, this is a good

00:04:58.000 --> 00:05:01.760
economy, service sector, etc. and it's being
shut down.

00:05:01.760 --> 00:05:04.010
And I see Trump fighting to open it.

00:05:04.010 --> 00:05:11.000
Is there some way in which that resonated
not just with Trump's uneducated white base,

00:05:11.000 --> 00:05:14.910
with the minority voters without a college
degree?

00:05:14.910 --> 00:05:15.910
Maybe so.

00:05:15.910 --> 00:05:18.190
But again, it would all be merely by chance.

00:05:18.190 --> 00:05:20.990
I think that that's the important thing to
keep in mind.

00:05:20.990 --> 00:05:27.040
Bottom line, we have every reason to believe
that this number of votes really was cast.

00:05:27.040 --> 00:05:30.660
Donald Trump did get millions more votes than
2016.

00:05:30.660 --> 00:05:35.830
Joe Biden got millions more votes than that
and received more votes for president than

00:05:35.830 --> 00:05:38.030
any candidate in history.

00:05:38.030 --> 00:05:47.340
And we will be analyzing those results and
thinking about them for much time 

00:05:47.340 --> 00:06:53.910
to come.

